WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:03.110
Okay, let's get into a audience questions
for the week.

00:00:03.110 --> 00:00:05.030
Three questions every week

00:00:05.030 --> 00:00:06.030
today.

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:07.030
Today's from Brian.

00:00:07.030 --> 00:00:10.510
Brian says, Hey David, what evidence leads
you to believe people are fundamentally or

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:17.169
generally good like you and Joe Rogan both
said on the Joe Rogan show?

00:00:17.169 --> 00:00:23.160
Well, so this is a very old philosophical
question, a question that exists even beyond

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:24.669
philosophy.

00:00:24.669 --> 00:00:26.079
Are we inherently good or bad?

00:00:26.079 --> 00:00:28.099
Uh, what about our greedy instincts?

00:00:28.099 --> 00:00:29.809
What about our selfless instincts?

00:00:29.809 --> 00:00:34.550
What about evolutionary advantages that one
or the other types of behavior conveys?

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:38.080
I don't know that I have anything particularly
new to add to it.

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:39.720
I'll, I'll just give you my take on it.

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:46.790
I believe people are generally what I would
call good, despite admittedly selfish and

00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:49.360
greedy instincts that many of us have.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:52.010
There's a few actual data points if you want
to look at it.

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:57.270
There are some pretty good studies from Harvard
and Yale about what are called first instincts

00:00:57.270 --> 00:00:59.900
in a variety of different tests.

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:07.259
And people's instincts are overall tend to
be towards cooperation, uh, even if selfishness

00:01:07.259 --> 00:01:12.210
is in the background as a motive sometimes
for that cooperation.

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:16.950
And that brings us to what I think are some
of the more compelling, um, uh, sort of data

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:20.030
points on this, which are evolutionary advantages.

00:01:20.030 --> 00:01:25.829
Humans that better cooperate historically
have done best.

00:01:25.829 --> 00:01:34.680
So in a sense, there has been a natural selection
towards cooperation and people that are, um,

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:42.789
uh, sort of open to acting in a way that benefits
their group even if underlying that are selfish

00:01:42.789 --> 00:01:44.299
or self centered interests.

00:01:44.299 --> 00:01:49.299
And I think one of the really interesting
things about this is, uh, a lot of the history

00:01:49.299 --> 00:01:52.399
on the evolutionary role of religion.

00:01:52.399 --> 00:01:58.740
And although I am not religious myself and
I believe that modern religion is rife with

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:05.930
problematic ideas, dogmatic, blindly dogmatic
thinking, uh, defenses of violence justified

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:07.140
by sacred tax.

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:10.900
I mean, all the people who watched the show
know my position on this.

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:17.660
I actually do believe that there are many
points in history where religious belief conferred

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:25.090
and evolutionary advantage where if you were
not in the in religious group, it was less

00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:30.200
likely that there would be sort of cooperative
relationships with the people you were living

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:33.150
with, whether it's in a village or or wherever
else.

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:37.190
Um, and that, uh, those people tended to not
do as well.

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:45.010
So yes, selfish instincts exist, but over
time, nature in a sense has selected for cooperative

00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:46.010
tendencies.

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:51.150
Even if those tendencies are rooted in some
longterm plan that is selfish.

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:57.250
In other words, well, I will act in a way
that is cooperative because I believe that

00:02:57.250 --> 00:03:02.050
that will actually better serve me personally
in the long run.

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:03.440
While you're still cooperating.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:08.970
In that sense, there are also studies on babies
which have found that at really early points

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:13.210
where we can even get a sense of what is going
on in the baby's mind.

00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:16.900
Babies have an instinct for fairness over
unfairness.

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:22.120
As soon as they can recognize it and they
seem to prefer good over evil.

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:25.990
They don't like unfairness in puppet shows
at very early ages.

00:03:25.990 --> 00:03:32.600
They tend to away from violence at very early
ages and in the end, statistically, most people

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:36.850
are not violent people who are committing
acts of violence in the real world.

00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:39.720
Most people are not criminals as defined by
society.

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:45.030
Most people lie mostly only in the sense of
white lies.

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:51.040
People do not like and react positively to
new use of criminality or violence or war

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:56.200
or terrorism or conflict, and certainly they're
not participating in it themselves.

00:03:56.200 --> 00:04:00.319
So of course reality is complex.

00:04:00.319 --> 00:04:03.250
Evil exists to the extent that that's what
we want to call it.

00:04:03.250 --> 00:04:09.700
Radicalization exists, but I believe people,
most people are fundamentally and generally

00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:10.700
good.

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:16.060
It's been not only informed by my personal
experience, but also by all of the different

00:04:16.060 --> 00:04:17.640
data points that I've mentioned here.

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:22.230
Uh, all of which are widely available for
you to find disagree with me, let me know.

00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:24.000
Are, are people fundamentally not good?

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:27.200
Are People fundamentally bad or evil or violent
or whatever?

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:28.593
Uh, I'd love to hear from you about this next
question today.

